[{"input":"# Lecture 6 – Algorithms and Programming Fundamentals\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this lecture, students should be able to:\n- Explain what an algorithm is.\n- Understand the principles of computational thinking.\n- Read and write simple pseudocode.\n- Interpret basic flowcharts.\n- Understand variables and data types.\n- Explain expressions and assignments.\n- Understand conditions and loops.\n- Explain the purpose of functions.\n- Recognize common data structures.\n- Understand why algorithm efficiency matters.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapter, we learned how software is transformed into machine instructions.\n\nHowever, before writing software, a programmer must answer a more fundamental question:\n\n> How do we describe a solution to a problem?\n\nComputer science is not primarily about programming languages.\n\nIt is about:\n- Solving problems\n- Organizing information\n- Designing processes\n\nProgramming languages are merely tools used to express those solutions.\n\nThe first step in software development is therefore designing an algorithm.\n\n## What Is an Algorithm?\n\nAn algorithm is a finite sequence of instructions that solves a problem or performs a task.\n\nAn algorithm must be:\n- Precise\n- Unambiguous\n- Executable\n- Finite\n\n### Everyday Examples\n\nMany tasks can be described as algorithms.\n\n#### Making Coffee\n\n```text\n1. Fill the kettle with water.\n2. Boil the water.\n3. Place coffee in a cup.\n4. Pour hot water.\n5. Stir.\n```\n\n#### Finding a Name in a Contact List\n\n```text\n1. Open contacts.\n2. Enter the name.\n3. Read matching results.\n```\n\n### Important Observation\n\nAlgorithms are not limited to computers.\nPeople follow algorithms every day.\nComputers simply execute them extremely quickly.\n\n## Computational Thinking\n\nComputational thinking is a way of analyzing problems so that they can be solved systematically.\n\n### Decomposition\n\nBreak a large problem into smaller parts.\n\n#### Example\n\nBuilding a website can be divided into:\n- Design\n- Content\n- Programming\n- Testing\n- Deployment\n\nSmaller problems are usually easier to solve.\n\n### Pattern Recognition\n\nIdentify similarities between problems.\n\n#### Example\n\nMany online stores use similar functions:\n- Product search\n- Shopping cart\n- Payment system\n\nRecognizing patterns allows reuse of existing solutions.\n\n### Abstraction\n\nFocus on important aspects.\nIgnore unnecessary details.\n\n#### Example\n\nA metro map shows:\n- Stations\n- Connections\n\nbut not:\n- Buildings\n- Trees\n- Street furniture\n\nAbstraction simplifies understanding.\n\n### Algorithm Design\n\nDevelop a sequence of steps that solves the problem.\nThis is where programming begins.","output":"","result":"# Lecture 6 – Algorithms and Programming Fundamentals\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this lecture, students should be able to:\n- Explain what an algorithm is.\n- Understand the principles of computational thinking.\n- Read and write simple pseudocode.\n- Interpret basic flowcharts.\n- Understand variables and data types.\n- Explain expressions and assignments.\n- Understand conditions and loops.\n- Explain the purpose of functions.\n- Recognize common data structures.\n- Understand why algorithm efficiency matters.\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapter, we learned how software is transformed into machine instructions.\n\nHowever, before writing software, a programmer must answer a more fundamental question:\n\n> How do we describe a solution to a problem?\n\nComputer science is not primarily about programming languages.\n\nIt is about:\n- Solving problems\n- Organizing information\n- Designing processes\n\nProgramming languages are merely tools used to express those solutions.\n\nThe first step in software development is therefore designing an algorithm.\n\n## What Is an Algorithm?\n\nAn algorithm is a finite sequence of instructions that solves a problem or performs a task.\n\nAn algorithm must be:\n- Precise\n- Unambiguous\n- Executable\n- Finite\n\n### Everyday Examples\n\nMany tasks can be described as algorithms.\n\n#### Making Coffee\n\n```text\n1. Fill the kettle with water.\n2. Boil the water.\n3. Place coffee in a cup.\n4. Pour hot water.\n5. Stir.\n```\n\n#### Finding a Name in a Contact List\n\n```text\n1. Open contacts.\n2. Enter the name.\n3. Read matching results.\n```\n\n### Important Observation\n\nAlgorithms are not limited to computers.\nPeople follow algorithms every day.\nComputers simply execute them extremely quickly.\n\n## Computational Thinking\n\nComputational thinking is a way of analyzing problems so that they can be solved systematically.\n\n### Decomposition\n\nBreak a large problem into smaller parts.\n\n#### Example\n\nBuilding a website can be divided into:\n- Design\n- Content\n- Programming\n- Testing\n- Deployment\n\nSmaller problems are usually easier to solve.\n\n### Pattern Recognition\n\nIdentify similarities between problems.\n\n#### Example\n\nMany online stores use similar functions:\n- Product search\n- Shopping cart\n- Payment system\n\nRecognizing patterns allows reuse of existing solutions.\n\n### Abstraction\n\nFocus on important aspects.\nIgnore unnecessary details.\n\n#### Example\n\nA metro map shows:\n- Stations\n- Connections\n\nbut not:\n- Buildings\n- Trees\n- Street furniture\n\nAbstraction simplifies understanding.\n\n### Algorithm Design\n\nDevelop a sequence of steps that solves the problem.\nThis is where programming begins.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-lecture-6-algorithms-and-programming-fundamentals\">\n<h1>Lecture 6 – Algorithms and Programming Fundamentals</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-learning-objectives\">\n<h1>Learning Objectives</h1>\n<p>At the end of this lecture, students should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain what an algorithm is.</li>\n<li>Understand the principles of computational thinking.</li>\n<li>Read and write simple pseudocode.</li>\n<li>Interpret basic flowcharts.</li>\n<li>Understand variables and data types.</li>\n<li>Explain expressions and assignments.</li>\n<li>Understand conditions and loops.</li>\n<li>Explain the purpose of functions.</li>\n<li>Recognize common data structures.</li>\n<li>Understand why algorithm efficiency matters.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-introduction\">\n<h1>Introduction</h1>\n<p>In the previous chapter, we learned how software is transformed into machine instructions.</p>\n<p>However, before writing software, a programmer must answer a more fundamental question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do we describe a solution to a problem?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Computer science is not primarily about programming languages.</p>\n<p>It is about:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Solving problems</li>\n<li>Organizing information</li>\n<li>Designing processes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Programming languages are merely tools used to express those solutions.</p>\n<p>The first step in software development is therefore designing an algorithm.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-what-is-an-algorithm\">\n<h1>What Is an Algorithm?</h1>\n<p>An algorithm is a finite sequence of instructions that solves a problem or performs a task.</p>\n<p>An algorithm must be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Precise</li>\n<li>Unambiguous</li>\n<li>Executable</li>\n<li>Finite</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-everyday-examples\">\n<h1>Everyday Examples</h1>\n<p>Many tasks can be described as algorithms.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-making-coffee\">\n<h1>Making Coffee</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">1. Fill the kettle with water.\n2. Boil the water.\n3. Place coffee in a cup.\n4. Pour hot water.\n5. Stir.</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-finding-a-name-in-a-contact-list\">\n<h1>Finding a Name in a Contact List</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">1. Open contacts.\n2. Enter the name.\n3. Read matching results.</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-important-observation\">\n<h1>Important Observation</h1>\n<p>Algorithms are not limited to computers.\nPeople follow algorithms every day.\nComputers simply execute them extremely quickly.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-computational-thinking\">\n<h1>Computational Thinking</h1>\n<p>Computational thinking is a way of analyzing problems so that they can be solved systematically.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-decomposition\">\n<h1>Decomposition</h1>\n<p>Break a large problem into smaller parts.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>Building a website can be divided into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Design</li>\n<li>Content</li>\n<li>Programming</li>\n<li>Testing</li>\n<li>Deployment</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Smaller problems are usually easier to solve.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-pattern-recognition\">\n<h1>Pattern Recognition</h1>\n<p>Identify similarities between problems.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-example-2\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>Many online stores use similar functions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Product search</li>\n<li>Shopping cart</li>\n<li>Payment system</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Recognizing patterns allows reuse of existing solutions.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-abstraction\">\n<h1>Abstraction</h1>\n<p>Focus on important aspects.\nIgnore unnecessary details.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-example-3\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>A metro map shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stations</li>\n<li>Connections</li>\n</ul>\n<p>but not:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Buildings</li>\n<li>Trees</li>\n<li>Street furniture</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Abstraction simplifies understanding.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3413-algorithm-design\">\n<h1>Algorithm Design</h1>\n<p>Develop a sequence of steps that solves the problem.\nThis is where programming begins.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"94fc89ea239d6d13eb65f31412a71756b643132122ad7af081e0c493c96ee017","markdown":"# College 6 – Algoritmen en Programmeerfundamenten\n\n## Leerdoelen\n\nAan het einde van dit college moeten studenten in staat zijn om:\n- Uit te leggen wat een algoritme is.\n- De principes van computationeel denken te begrijpen.\n- Eenvoudige pseudocode te lezen en te schrijven.\n- Eenvoudige stroomdiagrammen te interpreteren.\n- Variabelen en datatypes te begrijpen.\n- Expressies en toekenningen uit te leggen.\n- Voorwaarden en lussen te begrijpen.\n- Het doel van functies uit te leggen.\n- Veelvoorkomende datastructuren te herkennen.\n- Te begrijpen waarom de efficiëntie van algoritmen belangrijk is.\n\n## Inleiding\n\nIn het vorige hoofdstuk hebben we geleerd hoe software wordt omgezet in machine-instructies.\n\nVoordat software geschreven wordt, moet een programmeur echter een meer fundamentele vraag beantwoorden:\n\n> Hoe beschrijven we een oplossing voor een probleem?\n\nInformatica gaat niet in de eerste plaats over programmeertalen.\n\nHet gaat over:\n- Het oplossen van problemen\n- Het organiseren van informatie\n- Het ontwerpen van processen\n\nProgrammeertalen zijn slechts hulpmiddelen om die oplossingen uit te drukken.\n\nDe eerste stap in softwareontwikkeling is daarom het ontwerpen van een algoritme.\n\n## Wat Is een Algoritme?\n\nEen algoritme is een eindige reeks instructies die een probleem oplost of een taak uitvoert.\n\nEen algoritme moet zijn:\n- Precies\n- Ondubbelzinnig\n- Uitvoerbaar\n- Eindig\n\n### Alledaagse Voorbeelden\n\nVeel taken kunnen worden beschreven als algoritmen.\n\n#### Koffie Zetten\n\n```text\n1. Fill the kettle with water.\n2. Boil the water.\n3. Place coffee in a cup.\n4. Pour hot water.\n5. Stir.\n```\n\n#### Een Naam Zoeken in een Contactenlijst\n\n```text\n1. Open contacts.\n2. Enter the name.\n3. Read matching results.\n```\n\n### Belangrijke Observatie\n\nAlgoritmen zijn niet beperkt tot computers.\nMensen volgen elke dag algoritmen.\nComputers voeren ze gewoon extreem snel uit.\n\n## Computationeel Denken\n\nComputationeel denken is een manier om problemen te analyseren zodat ze systematisch kunnen worden opgelost.\n\n### Decompositie\n\nSplits een groot probleem op in kleinere delen.\n\n#### Voorbeeld\n\nHet bouwen van een website kan worden onderverdeeld in:\n- Ontwerp\n- Inhoud\n- Programmeren\n- Testen\n- Implementatie\n\nKleinere problemen zijn meestal gemakkelijker op te lossen.\n\n### Patroonherkenning\n\nIdentificeer overeenkomsten tussen problemen.\n\n#### Voorbeeld\n\nVeel online winkels gebruiken vergelijkbare functies:\n- Productzoekfunctie\n- Winkelwagentje\n- Betaalsysteem\n\nHet herkennen van patronen maakt het hergebruik van bestaande oplossingen mogelijk.\n\n### Abstractie\n\nFocus op belangrijke aspecten.\nNegeer onnodige details.\n\n#### Voorbeeld\n\nEen metrokaart toont:\n- Stations\n- Verbindingen\n\nmaar niet:\n- Gebouwen\n- Bomen\n- Straatmeubilair\n\nAbstractie vereenvoudigt het begrip.\n\n### Algoritmeontwerp\n\nOntwikkel een reeks stappen die het probleem oplost.\nHier begint het programmeren.\n"},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"94fc89ea239d6d13eb65f31412a71756b643132122ad7af081e0c493c96ee017","markdown":"# Cours 6 – Algorithmes et fondamentaux de la programmation\n\n## Objectifs d'apprentissage\n\nÀ la fin de ce cours, les étudiants devraient être capables de :\n- Expliquer ce qu'est un algorithme.\n- Comprendre les principes de la pensée computationnelle.\n- Lire et écrire du pseudocode simple.\n- Interpréter des organigrammes de base.\n- Comprendre les variables et les types de données.\n- Expliquer les expressions et les affectations.\n- Comprendre les conditions et les boucles.\n- Expliquer le rôle des fonctions.\n- Reconnaître les structures de données courantes.\n- Comprendre pourquoi l'efficacité des algorithmes est importante.\n\n## Introduction\n\nDans le chapitre précédent, nous avons appris comment un logiciel est transformé en instructions machine.\n\nCependant, avant d'écrire un logiciel, un programmeur doit répondre à une question plus fondamentale :\n\n> Comment décrit-on la solution d'un problème ?\n\nL'informatique ne porte pas principalement sur les langages de programmation.\n\nElle porte sur :\n- La résolution de problèmes\n- L'organisation de l'information\n- La conception de processus\n\nLes langages de programmation ne sont que des outils utilisés pour exprimer ces solutions.\n\nLa première étape du développement logiciel consiste donc à concevoir un algorithme.\n\n## Qu'est-ce qu'un algorithme ?\n\nUn algorithme est une séquence finie d'instructions qui résout un problème ou accomplit une tâche.\n\nUn algorithme doit être :\n- Précis\n- Non ambigu\n- Exécutable\n- Fini\n\n### Exemples du quotidien\n\nDe nombreuses tâches peuvent être décrites comme des algorithmes.\n\n#### Faire du café\n\n```text\n1. Fill the kettle with water.\n2. Boil the water.\n3. Place coffee in a cup.\n4. Pour hot water.\n5. Stir.\n```\n\n#### Trouver un nom dans une liste de contacts\n\n```text\n1. Open contacts.\n2. Enter the name.\n3. Read matching results.\n```\n\n### Observation importante\n\nLes algorithmes ne se limitent pas aux ordinateurs.\nLes gens suivent des algorithmes tous les jours.\nLes ordinateurs se contentent de les exécuter extrêmement rapidement.\n\n## Pensée computationnelle\n\nLa pensée computationnelle est une manière d'analyser les problèmes afin qu'ils puissent être résolus de manière systématique.\n\n### Décomposition\n\nDiviser un grand problème en parties plus petites.\n\n#### Exemple\n\nLa création d'un site web peut être divisée en :\n- Conception\n- Contenu\n- Programmation\n- Tests\n- Déploiement\n\nLes problèmes plus petits sont généralement plus faciles à résoudre.\n\n### Reconnaissance de motifs\n\nIdentifier les similitudes entre les problèmes.\n\n#### Exemple\n\nDe nombreuses boutiques en ligne utilisent des fonctions similaires :\n- Recherche de produits\n- Panier d'achat\n- Système de paiement\n\nReconnaître des motifs permet de réutiliser des solutions existantes.\n\n### Abstraction\n\nSe concentrer sur les aspects importants.\nIgnorer les détails inutiles.\n\n#### Exemple\n\nUn plan de métro montre :\n- Les stations\n- Les correspondances\n\nmais pas :\n- Les bâtiments\n- Les arbres\n- Le mobilier urbain\n\nL'abstraction simplifie la compréhension.\n\n### Conception d'algorithmes\n\nÉlaborer une séquence d'étapes qui résout le problème.\nC'est là que commence la programmation."}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"94fc89ea239d6d13eb65f31412a71756b643132122ad7af081e0c493c96ee017","language":"English"}},{"input":"## Pseudocode\n\nBefore writing actual code, programmers often use pseudocode.\nPseudocode resembles programming but does not follow strict language rules.\n\n### Example\n\nProblem: Calculate the area of a rectangle.\n\n```text\nREAD width\nREAD height\n\narea = width × height\n\nDISPLAY area\n```\n\n### Advantages\n\nPseudocode is:\n- Easy to read\n- Language-independent\n- Useful for planning solutions\n\n### Another Example\n\nDetermine whether someone can vote.\n\n```text\nREAD age\n\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Can vote\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Cannot vote\"\n```","output":"","result":"## Pseudocode\n\nBefore writing actual code, programmers often use pseudocode.\nPseudocode resembles programming but does not follow strict language rules.\n\n### Example\n\nProblem: Calculate the area of a rectangle.\n\n```text\nREAD width\nREAD height\n\narea = width × height\n\nDISPLAY area\n```\n\n### Advantages\n\nPseudocode is:\n- Easy to read\n- Language-independent\n- Useful for planning solutions\n\n### Another Example\n\nDetermine whether someone can vote.\n\n```text\nREAD age\n\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Can vote\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Cannot vote\"\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3415-pseudocode\">\n<h1>Pseudocode</h1>\n<p>Before writing actual code, programmers often use pseudocode.\nPseudocode resembles programming but does not follow strict language rules.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3415-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<p>Problem: Calculate the area of a rectangle.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">READ width\nREAD height\n\narea = width × height\n\nDISPLAY area</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3415-advantages\">\n<h1>Advantages</h1>\n<p>Pseudocode is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Easy to read</li>\n<li>Language-independent</li>\n<li>Useful for planning solutions</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3415-another-example\">\n<h1>Another Example</h1>\n<p>Determine whether someone can vote.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">READ age\n\nIF age &gt;= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Can vote\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Cannot vote\"</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"231f00e77fba8f138e350207455e1aa5901de77e61d953237bc2aa9c2c76ff19","markdown":"## Pseudocode\n\nVoordat ze echte code schrijven, gebruiken programmeurs vaak pseudocode.\nPseudocode lijkt op programmeren, maar volgt geen strikte taalregels.\n\n### Voorbeeld\n\nProbleem: Bereken de oppervlakte van een rechthoek.\n\n```text\nREAD width\nREAD height\n\narea = width × height\n\nDISPLAY area\n```\n\n### Voordelen\n\nPseudocode is:\n- Gemakkelijk te lezen\n- Taalonafhankelijk\n- Nuttig voor het plannen van oplossingen\n\n### Nog een voorbeeld\n\nBepaal of iemand mag stemmen.\n\n```text\nREAD age\n\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Can vote\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Cannot vote\"\n```"},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"231f00e77fba8f138e350207455e1aa5901de77e61d953237bc2aa9c2c76ff19","markdown":"## Pseudocode\n\nAvant d'écrire du code réel, les programmeurs utilisent souvent du pseudocode.\nLe pseudocode ressemble à de la programmation mais ne suit pas de règles de langage strictes.\n\n### Exemple\n\nProblème : Calculer l'aire d'un rectangle.\n\n```text\nREAD width\nREAD height\n\narea = width × height\n\nDISPLAY area\n```\n\n### Avantages\n\nLe pseudocode est :\n- Facile à lire\n- Indépendant du langage\n- Utile pour planifier des solutions\n\n### Un autre exemple\n\nDéterminer si quelqu'un peut voter.\n\n```text\nREAD age\n\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Can vote\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Cannot vote\"\n```"}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"231f00e77fba8f138e350207455e1aa5901de77e61d953237bc2aa9c2c76ff19","language":"English"}},{"input":"## Flowcharts\n\nFlowcharts provide a graphical representation of an algorithm.\n\n### Common Symbols\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Oval | Start / End |\n| Rectangle | Process |\n| Diamond | Decision |\n| Parallelogram | Input / Output |\n\n### Example Flow\n\n{.wide}\n![](https://daafoor.com/assets/dropzone/uploads/tinymce_images/questions/21/answers/mceu_79343466811634936333600.png)\n\n### Why Use Flowcharts? \n\nFlowcharts help: \n\n- Visualize algorithms \n\n- Communicate ideas \n\n- Identify mistakes ","output":"","result":"## Flowcharts\n\nFlowcharts provide a graphical representation of an algorithm.\n\n### Common Symbols\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Oval | Start / End |\n| Rectangle | Process |\n| Diamond | Decision |\n| Parallelogram | Input / Output |\n\n### Example Flow\n\n{.wide}\n![](https://daafoor.com/assets/dropzone/uploads/tinymce_images/questions/21/answers/mceu_79343466811634936333600.png)\n\n### Why Use Flowcharts? \n\nFlowcharts help: \n\n- Visualize algorithms \n\n- Communicate ideas \n\n- Identify mistakes ","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3416-flowcharts\">\n<h1>Flowcharts</h1>\n<p>Flowcharts provide a graphical representation of an algorithm.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3416-common-symbols\">\n<h1>Common Symbols</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symbol</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Oval</td>\n<td>Start / End</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rectangle</td>\n<td>Process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diamond</td>\n<td>Decision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parallelogram</td>\n<td>Input / Output</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3416-example-flow\">\n<h1>Example Flow</h1>\n<figure class=\"wide\">\n<img src=\"https://daafoor.com/assets/dropzone/uploads/tinymce_images/questions/21/answers/mceu_79343466811634936333600.png\" alt=\"\">\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3416-why-use-flowcharts\">\n<h1>Why Use Flowcharts?</h1>\n<p>Flowcharts help:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Visualize algorithms</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Communicate ideas</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Identify mistakes</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"7ded08e5bb1272820a8510dfe1ec748b8afb29aef1172e6f4cd04ca2896dc81f","markdown":"## Stroomdiagrammen\n\nStroomdiagrammen geven een grafische weergave van een algoritme.\n\n### Veelgebruikte Symbolen\n\n| Symbool | Betekenis |\n|---|---|\n| Ovaal | Start / Einde |\n| Rechthoek | Proces |\n| Ruit | Beslissing |\n| Parallellogram | Invoer / Uitvoer |\n\n### Voorbeeld van een Stroomdiagram\n\n{.wide}\n![](https://daafoor.com/assets/dropzone/uploads/tinymce_images/questions/21/answers/mceu_79343466811634936333600.png)\n\n### Waarom Stroomdiagrammen Gebruiken?\n\nStroomdiagrammen helpen bij het:\n\n- Visualiseren van algoritmes\n\n- Communiceren van ideeën\n\n- Identificeren van fouten"},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"7ded08e5bb1272820a8510dfe1ec748b8afb29aef1172e6f4cd04ca2896dc81f","markdown":"## Organigrammes\n\nLes organigrammes fournissent une représentation graphique d'un algorithme.\n\n### Symboles courants\n\n| Symbole | Signification |\n|---|---|\n| Ovale | Début / Fin |\n| Rectangle | Processus |\n| Losange | Décision |\n| Parallélogramme | Entrée / Sortie |\n\n### Exemple d'organigramme\n\n{.wide}\n![](https://daafoor.com/assets/dropzone/uploads/tinymce_images/questions/21/answers/mceu_79343466811634936333600.png)\n\n### Pourquoi utiliser des organigrammes ? \n\nLes organigrammes aident à : \n\n- Visualiser les algorithmes \n\n- Communiquer des idées \n\n- Identifier les erreurs \n"}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"7ded08e5bb1272820a8510dfe1ec748b8afb29aef1172e6f4cd04ca2896dc81f","language":"English"}},{"input":"## Variables\n\nPrograms often need to store information.\nVariables provide named locations for storing data.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n```\n\nVariable: `age`\n\nValue: `20`\n\n### Analogy\n\nThink of a variable as a labeled box.\n\n```text\nBox label: age\nContents: 20\n```\n\nThe value can later change.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n\nage = 21\n```\n\nThe same variable now contains a different value.\n\n## Data Types\n\nDifferent kinds of information require different representations.\nThese categories are known as data types.\n\n### Integer\nWhole numbers.\n\n```text\n5\n-3\n42\n```\n\n### Real Number\n\n```text\n3.14\n12.5\n0.01\n```\n\n### Text (String)\n\n```text\n\"Ben\"\n\"Computer Science\"\n```\n\n### Boolean\n\n```text\nTRUE\nFALSE\n```\n\nBooleans are heavily used in decisions.\n\n## Expressions and Assignment\n\nPrograms frequently manipulate data.\nAn expression computes a value.\n\n```text\n5 + 3\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```text\n8\n```\n\n### Assignment\n\n```text\ntotal = 5 + 3\n```\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nprice = 100\ntax = 21\n\ntotal = price + tax\n```\n\nResult: `121`","output":"","result":"## Variables\n\nPrograms often need to store information.\nVariables provide named locations for storing data.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n```\n\nVariable: `age`\n\nValue: `20`\n\n### Analogy\n\nThink of a variable as a labeled box.\n\n```text\nBox label: age\nContents: 20\n```\n\nThe value can later change.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n\nage = 21\n```\n\nThe same variable now contains a different value.\n\n## Data Types\n\nDifferent kinds of information require different representations.\nThese categories are known as data types.\n\n### Integer\nWhole numbers.\n\n```text\n5\n-3\n42\n```\n\n### Real Number\n\n```text\n3.14\n12.5\n0.01\n```\n\n### Text (String)\n\n```text\n\"Ben\"\n\"Computer Science\"\n```\n\n### Boolean\n\n```text\nTRUE\nFALSE\n```\n\nBooleans are heavily used in decisions.\n\n## Expressions and Assignment\n\nPrograms frequently manipulate data.\nAn expression computes a value.\n\n```text\n5 + 3\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```text\n8\n```\n\n### Assignment\n\n```text\ntotal = 5 + 3\n```\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nprice = 100\ntax = 21\n\ntotal = price + tax\n```\n\nResult: `121`","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-variables\">\n<h1>Variables</h1>\n<p>Programs often need to store information.\nVariables provide named locations for storing data.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">age = 20</pre>\n<p>Variable: <code>age</code></p>\n<p>Value: <code>20</code></p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-analogy\">\n<h1>Analogy</h1>\n<p>Think of a variable as a labeled box.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Box label: age\nContents: 20</pre>\n<p>The value can later change.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">age = 20\n\nage = 21</pre>\n<p>The same variable now contains a different value.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-data-types\">\n<h1>Data Types</h1>\n<p>Different kinds of information require different representations.\nThese categories are known as data types.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-integer\">\n<h1>Integer</h1>\n<p>Whole numbers.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">5\n-3\n42</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-real-number\">\n<h1>Real Number</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">3.14\n12.5\n0.01</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-text-string\">\n<h1>Text (String)</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">\"Ben\"\n\"Computer Science\"</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-boolean\">\n<h1>Boolean</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">TRUE\nFALSE</pre>\n<p>Booleans are heavily used in decisions.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-expressions-and-assignment\">\n<h1>Expressions and Assignment</h1>\n<p>Programs frequently manipulate data.\nAn expression computes a value.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">5 + 3</pre>\n<p>Result:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">8</pre>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-assignment\">\n<h1>Assignment</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">total = 5 + 3</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3417-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">price = 100\ntax = 21\n\ntotal = price + tax</pre>\n<p>Result: <code>121</code></p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"35e04c9f8d544f3bb483aee41799f441324e7b49ebbfcc41c3ac22971ececb94","markdown":"## Variabelen\n\nProgramma's moeten vaak informatie opslaan.\nVariabelen bieden benoemde locaties voor het opslaan van gegevens.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n```\n\nVariabele: `age`\n\nWaarde: `20`\n\n### Analogie\n\nStel je een variabele voor als een gelabelde doos.\n\n```text\nBox label: age\nContents: 20\n```\n\nDe waarde kan later veranderen.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n\nage = 21\n```\n\nDezelfde variabele bevat nu een andere waarde.\n\n## Datatypes\n\nVerschillende soorten informatie vereisen verschillende representaties.\nDeze categorieën worden datatypes genoemd.\n\n### Geheel getal\nGehele getallen.\n\n```text\n5\n-3\n42\n```\n\n### Reëel getal\n\n```text\n3.14\n12.5\n0.01\n```\n\n### Tekst (String)\n\n```text\n\"Ben\"\n\"Computer Science\"\n```\n\n### Booleaanse waarde\n\n```text\nTRUE\nFALSE\n```\n\nBoolean worden veel gebruikt bij beslissingen.\n\n## Expressies en toekenning\n\nProgramma's manipuleren vaak gegevens.\nEen expressie berekent een waarde.\n\n```text\n5 + 3\n```\n\nResultaat:\n\n```text\n8\n```\n\n### Toekenning\n\n```text\ntotal = 5 + 3\n```\n\n### Voorbeeld\n\n```text\nprice = 100\ntax = 21\n\ntotal = price + tax\n```\n\nResultaat: `121`"},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"35e04c9f8d544f3bb483aee41799f441324e7b49ebbfcc41c3ac22971ececb94","markdown":"## Variables\n\nLes programmes doivent souvent stocker des informations.\nLes variables fournissent des emplacements nommés pour stocker des données.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n```\n\nVariable : `age`\n\nValeur : `20`\n\n### Analogie\n\nImaginez une variable comme une boîte étiquetée.\n\n```text\nBox label: age\nContents: 20\n```\n\nLa valeur peut changer par la suite.\n\n```text\nage = 20\n\nage = 21\n```\n\nLa même variable contient maintenant une valeur différente.\n\n## Types de données\n\nDifférents types d'informations nécessitent des représentations différentes.\nCes catégories sont appelées types de données.\n\n### Entier\nNombres entiers.\n\n```text\n5\n-3\n42\n```\n\n### Nombre réel\n\n```text\n3.14\n12.5\n0.01\n```\n\n### Texte (chaîne de caractères)\n\n```text\n\"Ben\"\n\"Computer Science\"\n```\n\n### Booléen\n\n```text\nTRUE\nFALSE\n```\n\nLes booléens sont fortement utilisés dans les décisions.\n\n## Expressions et affectation\n\nLes programmes manipulent fréquemment des données.\nUne expression calcule une valeur.\n\n```text\n5 + 3\n```\n\nRésultat :\n\n```text\n8\n```\n\n### Affectation\n\n```text\ntotal = 5 + 3\n```\n\n### Exemple\n\n```text\nprice = 100\ntax = 21\n\ntotal = price + tax\n```\n\nRésultat : `121`"}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"35e04c9f8d544f3bb483aee41799f441324e7b49ebbfcc41c3ac22971ececb94","language":"English"}},{"input":"## Input and Output\n\nPrograms typically interact with users.\n\n### Input\n- Name\n- Age\n- Password\n- Temperature\n\n### Output\n- Messages\n- Results\n- Reports\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nREAD name\n\nDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nDISPLAY name\n```\n\n## Conditions\n\nPrograms often make decisions.\n\n```text\nIF temperature > 30\n    DISPLAY \"Hot\"\n```\n\n### Complete Example\n\n```text\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Adult\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Minor\"\n```\n\n## Comparison Operators\n\n| Operator | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| = | Equal |\n| ≠ | Not equal |\n| > | Greater than |\n| < | Less than |\n| >= | Greater than or equal |\n| <= | Less than or equal |\n\n## Loops\n\n```text\nFOR number = 1 TO 5\n    DISPLAY number\nEND\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```","output":"","result":"## Input and Output\n\nPrograms typically interact with users.\n\n### Input\n- Name\n- Age\n- Password\n- Temperature\n\n### Output\n- Messages\n- Results\n- Reports\n\n### Example\n\n```text\nREAD name\n\nDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nDISPLAY name\n```\n\n## Conditions\n\nPrograms often make decisions.\n\n```text\nIF temperature > 30\n    DISPLAY \"Hot\"\n```\n\n### Complete Example\n\n```text\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Adult\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Minor\"\n```\n\n## Comparison Operators\n\n| Operator | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| = | Equal |\n| ≠ | Not equal |\n| > | Greater than |\n| < | Less than |\n| >= | Greater than or equal |\n| <= | Less than or equal |\n\n## Loops\n\n```text\nFOR number = 1 TO 5\n    DISPLAY number\nEND\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-input-and-output\">\n<h1>Input and Output</h1>\n<p>Programs typically interact with users.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-input\">\n<h1>Input</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Name</li>\n<li>Age</li>\n<li>Password</li>\n<li>Temperature</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-output\">\n<h1>Output</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Messages</li>\n<li>Results</li>\n<li>Reports</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">READ name\n\nDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nDISPLAY name</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-conditions\">\n<h1>Conditions</h1>\n<p>Programs often make decisions.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">IF temperature &gt; 30\n    DISPLAY \"Hot\"</pre>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-complete-example\">\n<h1>Complete Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">IF age &gt;= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Adult\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Minor\"</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-comparison-operators\">\n<h1>Comparison Operators</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>=</td>\n<td>Equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>≠</td>\n<td>Not equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt;</td>\n<td>Greater than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;</td>\n<td>Less than</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&gt;=</td>\n<td>Greater than or equal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&lt;=</td>\n<td>Less than or equal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3418-loops\">\n<h1>Loops</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">FOR number = 1 TO 5\n    DISPLAY number\nEND</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">1\n2\n3\n4\n5</pre>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"647918ed6346767d63be8e1cf236130c2d4efdcd049dac7c723dda7a5a1e9e86","markdown":"## Invoer en Uitvoer\n\nProgramma's communiceren meestal met gebruikers.\n\n### Invoer\n- Naam\n- Leeftijd\n- Wachtwoord\n- Temperatuur\n\n### Uitvoer\n- Berichten\n- Resultaten\n- Rapporten\n\n### Voorbeeld\n\n```text\nREAD name\n\nDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nDISPLAY name\n```\n\n## Voorwaarden\n\nProgramma's nemen vaak beslissingen.\n\n```text\nIF temperature > 30\n    DISPLAY \"Hot\"\n```\n\n### Volledig Voorbeeld\n\n```text\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Adult\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Minor\"\n```\n\n## Vergelijkingsoperatoren\n\n| Operator | Betekenis |\n|---|---|\n| = | Gelijk aan |\n| ≠ | Niet gelijk aan |\n| > | Groter dan |\n| < | Kleiner dan |\n| >= | Groter dan of gelijk aan |\n| <= | Kleiner dan of gelijk aan |\n\n## Lussen\n\n```text\nFOR number = 1 TO 5\n    DISPLAY number\nEND\n```\n\nUitvoer:\n\n```text\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```"},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"647918ed6346767d63be8e1cf236130c2d4efdcd049dac7c723dda7a5a1e9e86","markdown":"## Entrée et sortie\n\nLes programmes interagissent généralement avec les utilisateurs.\n\n### Entrée\n- Nom\n- Âge\n- Mot de passe\n- Température\n\n### Sortie\n- Messages\n- Résultats\n- Rapports\n\n### Exemple\n\n```text\nREAD name\n\nDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nDISPLAY name\n```\n\n## Conditions\n\nLes programmes prennent souvent des décisions.\n\n```text\nIF temperature > 30\n    DISPLAY \"Hot\"\n```\n\n### Exemple complet\n\n```text\nIF age >= 18\n    DISPLAY \"Adult\"\nELSE\n    DISPLAY \"Minor\"\n```\n\n## Opérateurs de comparaison\n\n| Opérateur | Signification |\n|---|---|\n| = | Égal |\n| ≠ | Différent |\n| > | Supérieur à |\n| < | Inférieur à |\n| >= | Supérieur ou égal |\n| <= | Inférieur ou égal |\n\n## Boucles\n\n```text\nFOR number = 1 TO 5\n    DISPLAY number\nEND\n```\n\nSortie :\n\n```text\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n```"}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"647918ed6346767d63be8e1cf236130c2d4efdcd049dac7c723dda7a5a1e9e86","language":"English"}},{"input":"## Functions\n\nA function is a reusable block of instructions designed to perform a specific task.\n\n```text\nFUNCTION greet\n\tDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nCalling:\n\n```text\ngreet()\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nHello\n```\n\n## Parameters and Return Values\n\n```text\nFUNCTION square(number)\n\tRETURN number × number\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nCall:\n\n```text\nsquare(4)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n16\n```\n\n## Data Structures\n\n### Arrays\n\nAn array stores multiple values of the same type.\n\n### Lists\n\nLists are similar to arrays but more flexible.\n\n### Stacks\n\nLast In, First Out (LIFO).\n\n### Queues\n\nFirst In, First Out (FIFO).\n\n### Why Data Structures Matter\n\nChoosing the right data structure can significantly improve performance.\n\n## Algorithm Efficiency\n\nTwo algorithms may solve the same problem but require different amounts of:\n- Time\n- Memory\n\nA major goal of computer science is not merely solving problems, but solving them efficiently.","output":"","result":"## Functions\n\nA function is a reusable block of instructions designed to perform a specific task.\n\n```text\nFUNCTION greet\n\tDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nCalling:\n\n```text\ngreet()\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nHello\n```\n\n## Parameters and Return Values\n\n```text\nFUNCTION square(number)\n\tRETURN number × number\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nCall:\n\n```text\nsquare(4)\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n16\n```\n\n## Data Structures\n\n### Arrays\n\nAn array stores multiple values of the same type.\n\n### Lists\n\nLists are similar to arrays but more flexible.\n\n### Stacks\n\nLast In, First Out (LIFO).\n\n### Queues\n\nFirst In, First Out (FIFO).\n\n### Why Data Structures Matter\n\nChoosing the right data structure can significantly improve performance.\n\n## Algorithm Efficiency\n\nTwo algorithms may solve the same problem but require different amounts of:\n- Time\n- Memory\n\nA major goal of computer science is not merely solving problems, but solving them efficiently.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-functions\">\n<h1>Functions</h1>\n<p>A function is a reusable block of instructions designed to perform a specific task.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">FUNCTION greet\n\tDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nEND FUNCTION</pre>\n<p>Calling:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">greet()</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Hello</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-parameters-and-return-values\">\n<h1>Parameters and Return Values</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">FUNCTION square(number)\n\tRETURN number × number\nEND FUNCTION</pre>\n<p>Call:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">square(4)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">16</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-data-structures\">\n<h1>Data Structures</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-arrays\">\n<h1>Arrays</h1>\n<p>An array stores multiple values of the same type.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-lists\">\n<h1>Lists</h1>\n<p>Lists are similar to arrays but more flexible.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-stacks\">\n<h1>Stacks</h1>\n<p>Last In, First Out (LIFO).</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-queues\">\n<h1>Queues</h1>\n<p>First In, First Out (FIFO).</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-why-data-structures-matter\">\n<h1>Why Data Structures Matter</h1>\n<p>Choosing the right data structure can significantly improve performance.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3419-algorithm-efficiency\">\n<h1>Algorithm Efficiency</h1>\n<p>Two algorithms may solve the same problem but require different amounts of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Time</li>\n<li>Memory</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A major goal of computer science is not merely solving problems, but solving them efficiently.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{"Dutch":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"b857b422b6ae5b3551ebe048bd622c3c97def84f7c9ae1bdbb035a7783aaf909","markdown":"## Functies\n\nEen functie is een herbruikbaar blok instructies dat is ontworpen om een specifieke taak uit te voeren.\n\n```text\nFUNCTION greet\n\tDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nAanroepen:\n\n```text\ngreet()\n```\n\nUitvoer:\n\n```text\nHello\n```\n\n## Parameters en Retourwaarden\n\n```text\nFUNCTION square(number)\n\tRETURN number × number\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nAanroep:\n\n```text\nsquare(4)\n```\n\nUitvoer:\n\n```text\n16\n```\n\n## Datastructuren\n\n### Arrays\n\nEen array slaat meerdere waarden van hetzelfde type op.\n\n### Lijsten\n\nLijsten lijken op arrays, maar zijn flexibeler.\n\n### Stacks\n\nLast In, First Out (LIFO).\n\n### Wachtrijen\n\nFirst In, First Out (FIFO).\n\n### Waarom Datastructuren Belangrijk Zijn\n\nHet kiezen van de juiste datastructuur kan de prestaties aanzienlijk verbeteren.\n\n## Algoritme-efficiëntie\n\nTwee algoritmen kunnen hetzelfde probleem oplossen, maar verschillende hoeveelheden van het volgende vereisen:\n- Tijd\n- Geheugen\n\nEen belangrijk doel van de informatica is niet enkel het oplossen van problemen, maar dit ook efficiënt te doen."},"French":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"b857b422b6ae5b3551ebe048bd622c3c97def84f7c9ae1bdbb035a7783aaf909","markdown":"## Fonctions\n\nUne fonction est un bloc d'instructions réutilisable conçu pour effectuer une tâche spécifique.\n\n```text\nFUNCTION greet\n\tDISPLAY \"Hello\"\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nAppel :\n\n```text\ngreet()\n```\n\nSortie :\n\n```text\nHello\n```\n\n## Paramètres et valeurs de retour\n\n```text\nFUNCTION square(number)\n\tRETURN number × number\nEND FUNCTION\n```\n\nAppel :\n\n```text\nsquare(4)\n```\n\nSortie :\n\n```text\n16\n```\n\n## Structures de données\n\n### Tableaux\n\nUn tableau stocke plusieurs valeurs du même type.\n\n### Listes\n\nLes listes sont similaires aux tableaux mais plus flexibles.\n\n### Piles\n\nDernier entré, premier sorti (LIFO).\n\n### Files\n\nPremier entré, premier sorti (FIFO).\n\n### Pourquoi les structures de données sont importantes\n\nChoisir la bonne structure de données peut considérablement améliorer les performances.\n\n## Efficacité des algorithmes\n\nDeux algorithmes peuvent résoudre le même problème mais nécessiter des quantités différentes de :\n- Temps\n- Mémoire\n\nUn objectif majeur de l'informatique n'est pas seulement de résoudre des problèmes, mais de les résoudre efficacement."}},"translationSource":{"format":3,"sourceHash":"b857b422b6ae5b3551ebe048bd622c3c97def84f7c9ae1bdbb035a7783aaf909","language":"English"}},{"input":"## Summary\n\n- Algorithms are step-by-step procedures for solving problems.\n- Computational thinking involves decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction, and algorithm design.\n- Pseudocode and flowcharts help describe solutions.\n- Variables store information.\n- Data types define the kinds of values that can be stored.\n- Conditions allow programs to make decisions.\n- Loops allow programs to repeat actions.\n- Functions support modular and reusable software.\n- Arrays, lists, stacks, and queues are common data structures.\n- Algorithm efficiency becomes increasingly important as problem size grows.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n{.wide}\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Algorithm | Sequence of steps solving a problem |\n| Computational Thinking | Structured approach to problem solving |\n| Pseudocode | Informal description of an algorithm |\n| Flowchart | Graphical representation of an algorithm |\n| Variable | Named storage location |\n| Data Type | Category of data values |\n| Expression | Computation producing a value |\n| Assignment | Storing a value in a variable |\n| Condition | Decision-making construct |\n| Loop | Repetition construct |\n| Function | Reusable block of instructions |\n| Parameter | Input to a function |\n| Return Value | Output from a function |\n| Array | Ordered collection of values |\n| List | Flexible collection of values |\n| Stack | Last-In First-Out structure |\n| Queue | First-In First-Out structure |\n\n## Review Questions\n\n- What is an algorithm?\n- What are the four components of computational thinking?\n- Why is pseudocode useful?\n- What is the purpose of a flowchart?\n- What is a variable?\n- Give examples of four common data types.\n- What is the difference between an expression and an assignment?\n- How do conditions influence program execution?\n- Why are loops important?\n- What are the advantages of functions?\n- What is the difference between a stack and a queue?\n- Why do data structures matter?\n- Why can one algorithm be better than another even if both produce the correct result?\n\n## Transition to Lecture 7\n\nWe now understand how software is designed and structured. However, modern programs do not run directly on hardware. They rely on a powerful layer of software that manages resources, memory, devices, and applications.\n\nIn the next lecture, we will study the **Operating System**, one of the most important pieces of software in any computer system.","output":"","result":"## Summary\n\n- Algorithms are step-by-step procedures for solving problems.\n- Computational thinking involves decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction, and algorithm design.\n- Pseudocode and flowcharts help describe solutions.\n- Variables store information.\n- Data types define the kinds of values that can be stored.\n- Conditions allow programs to make decisions.\n- Loops allow programs to repeat actions.\n- Functions support modular and reusable software.\n- Arrays, lists, stacks, and queues are common data structures.\n- Algorithm efficiency becomes increasingly important as problem size grows.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n{.wide}\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Algorithm | Sequence of steps solving a problem |\n| Computational Thinking | Structured approach to problem solving |\n| Pseudocode | Informal description of an algorithm |\n| Flowchart | Graphical representation of an algorithm |\n| Variable | Named storage location |\n| Data Type | Category of data values |\n| Expression | Computation producing a value |\n| Assignment | Storing a value in a variable |\n| Condition | Decision-making construct |\n| Loop | Repetition construct |\n| Function | Reusable block of instructions |\n| Parameter | Input to a function |\n| Return Value | Output from a function |\n| Array | Ordered collection of values |\n| List | Flexible collection of values |\n| Stack | Last-In First-Out structure |\n| Queue | First-In First-Out structure |\n\n## Review Questions\n\n- What is an algorithm?\n- What are the four components of computational thinking?\n- Why is pseudocode useful?\n- What is the purpose of a flowchart?\n- What is a variable?\n- Give examples of four common data types.\n- What is the difference between an expression and an assignment?\n- How do conditions influence program execution?\n- Why are loops important?\n- What are the advantages of functions?\n- What is the difference between a stack and a queue?\n- Why do data structures matter?\n- Why can one algorithm be better than another even if both produce the correct result?\n\n## Transition to Lecture 7\n\nWe now understand how software is designed and structured. However, modern programs do not run directly on hardware. They rely on a powerful layer of software that manages resources, memory, devices, and applications.\n\nIn the next lecture, we will study the **Operating System**, one of the most important pieces of software in any computer system.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3420-summary\">\n<h1>Summary</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Algorithms are step-by-step procedures for solving problems.</li>\n<li>Computational thinking involves decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction, and algorithm design.</li>\n<li>Pseudocode and flowcharts help describe solutions.</li>\n<li>Variables store information.</li>\n<li>Data types define the kinds of values that can be stored.</li>\n<li>Conditions allow programs to make decisions.</li>\n<li>Loops allow programs to repeat actions.</li>\n<li>Functions support modular and reusable software.</li>\n<li>Arrays, lists, stacks, and queues are common data structures.</li>\n<li>Algorithm efficiency becomes increasingly important as problem size grows.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3420-key-terms\">\n<h1>Key Terms</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\" class=\"wide\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Algorithm</td>\n<td>Sequence of steps solving a problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Computational Thinking</td>\n<td>Structured approach to problem solving</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pseudocode</td>\n<td>Informal description of an algorithm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flowchart</td>\n<td>Graphical representation of an algorithm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Variable</td>\n<td>Named storage location</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data Type</td>\n<td>Category of data values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expression</td>\n<td>Computation producing a value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assignment</td>\n<td>Storing a value in a variable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Condition</td>\n<td>Decision-making construct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loop</td>\n<td>Repetition construct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Function</td>\n<td>Reusable block of instructions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parameter</td>\n<td>Input to a function</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Return Value</td>\n<td>Output from a function</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Array</td>\n<td>Ordered collection of values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>List</td>\n<td>Flexible collection of values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stack</td>\n<td>Last-In First-Out structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Queue</td>\n<td>First-In First-Out structure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3420-review-questions\">\n<h1>Review Questions</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>What is an algorithm?</li>\n<li>What are the four components of computational thinking?</li>\n<li>Why is pseudocode useful?</li>\n<li>What is the purpose of a flowchart?</li>\n<li>What is a variable?</li>\n<li>Give examples of four common data types.</li>\n<li>What is the difference between an expression and an assignment?</li>\n<li>How do conditions influence program execution?</li>\n<li>Why are loops important?</li>\n<li>What are the advantages of functions?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between a stack and a queue?</li>\n<li>Why do data structures matter?</li>\n<li>Why can one algorithm be better than another even if both produce the correct result?</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture06-c3420-transition-to-lecture-7\">\n<h1>Transition to Lecture 7</h1>\n<p>We now understand how software is designed and structured. However, modern programs do not run directly on hardware. They rely on a powerful layer of software that manages resources, memory, devices, and applications.</p>\n<p>In the next lecture, we will study the <strong>Operating System</strong>, one of the most important pieces of software in any computer system.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]